ter his flaws become a lot more  apparent and a big crux of the character  is him not understanding the feelings of  those around him the best example of  that being of course killer past the  zeldic family arc gone is completely  oblivious to killer's emotions and it  just doesn't quite gel with the  character that we're given in chapter  one so in that way the 2011 anime gone  is a bit more consistent however this is  far from a perfect seamless change and  it does definitely make some things a  bit more difficult to reconcile for  example a lot of people often question  why gon is so determined to find jing or  why he doesn't resent his father for  leaving him and the initial offering of  the manga sums all of that up quite  nicely gone isn't capable of resenting  his father because it wasn't jing's  fault that he had to leave once again it  was technically mito's fault and sure  she obviously had gone's best interest  at heart but that doesn't change the  action itself plus with a lifetime of  having his father forcibly removed from  his life all of a sudden it does make a  lot of sense to want to go and find him  although that aspect is far more  prevalently pushed by the kite flashback  so it's a tricky issue to adjudicate  because both parts have their advantages  and their pitfalls what the manga did  was a stock standard cohesive setup  where gone's motivations make 100 solid  sense and it's very easy to embark on  that journey however what the anime did  was more accurate to what the story was  going to become and i do think it does a  better job of making the realities of  the world itself a little bit more  cohesive as well as highlighting the  bond between gonomito so as radical of a  change as it is i do think it was  probably the right decision to change  this one massive conversation and sure  you can argue that hey this isn't fair  on jing he gets a pretty bad rap for the  whole child abandonment thing but that  is the man that togashi eventually chose  to create all this change really does is  remove that initial illusion and it  gives the anime audience a much more  accurate sense of what to expect from  jing because this world has no heroes  and whatever jing was meant to be when  togashi began the series has no place in  what it became but that still does leave  us with the rather annoying question of  did jing abandon gone because  canonically i guess no no he didn't i  think i mean this is one of the big  problems with a writing style like  togashi's because his naturally  developing garden doesn't necessarily  reflect the seeds that he used and i did  say at the beginning of this video that  the answer to this question depends  entirely on which medium you consumed in  hunter hunter but it's actually more  complicated than even that even if we  restrict our focus to the manga alone  there are two very different stories  being told here and sometimes you simply  can't take what togashi was setting up  in the early days as set in stone gospel  in fact you can't really take anything  as set in stone because tagashi is a man  whose mind will never settle he just  keeps moving from idea to idea to idea  and he's much more interested in playing  out those scenarios than he is in  strictly enforcing the canon of the  world which is fine by the way i mean  i'd probably give a lot of other authors  a pretty hard time about that kind of  thing but in togashi's case it works  much more in favor of the series than it  does against it but ultimately did jing  abandon gone well no no he didn't but  also yes yes he did i mean pretty much  no but also mostly yes and mostly yes is  also the answer to our question of  whether or not you would like another  video because there's always more to  learn explore and experience with this  wonderful series so i look forward to  seeing you there

